Kind: widen
Issue: #954
Holds open: wakes the high thread, bound to the waker's processor and above it, between the wakee's wakeup and the look at handoff_wakee, and spins there for 50 ms or until the high thread is done, so that a tick in the window may let the high thread take the processor from the waker, every time
Run: --entry 67 300 -smp 4
Premise: none -- its hold is on the path every time the test drives it, and nothing a boot does can skip it: the test's own lines, which the harness asks for, say the path was driven

diff --git a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/cpu/wakee_test.c b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/cpu/wakee_test.c
index 5d13982f..8ed07dbf 100644
--- a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/cpu/wakee_test.c
+++ b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/cpu/wakee_test.c
@@ -198,6 +198,15 @@ wakee_test(void)
 	thread_wakeup((event_t) &wakee_test_other_event);
 	took_first = self->handoff_wakee;
 	thread_wakeup((event_t) &wakee_test_wakee_event);
+	/* 954-widen-high-in-window: the high thread runnable here before the
+	 * look, and 50 ms of ticks at which it may take this processor */
+	thread_wakeup((event_t) &wakee_test_high_event);
+	{
+		uint64_t	w0 = rdtsc();
+
+		while (!wakee_test_high_done && rdtsc() - w0 < hz / 20)
+			cpu_pause();
+	}
 	took_named = self->handoff_wakee;
 	self->handoff_target = THREAD_NULL;
 	self->handoff_hint = FALSE;
